ection: a cancel mid-write can leave inconsistent state; the gate must ignore `cancelled`.\n\nVerdict: B per-ref + true? But note: with per-ref and cancel-in-progress true, the currently running run gets cancelled too — if a cancel mid-write leaves inconsistent state, cancelling the running run is dangerous. Hmm. Kimi's objection: cancel mid-write can leave inconsistent state; the gate must ignore `cancelled`. With `true`, the running run is killed — that's exactly the risk. With `false`, only waiting predecessors are deleted, so the mid-write risk is bounded to... actually with false and per-SHA, new SHAs queue up in the same group and the waiting ones are dropped — but the 7/8 cancelled happened in 10 minutes; with per-SHA groups they wouldn't cancel each other at all. Wait — if group is per-ref with false, then new runs in the same group cancel *pending* ones (that's the standard behavior: cancel-in-progress false cancels queued runs of older revisions). The measured 7 cancelled in 8 runs under the current setting (per-ref, false) means queued predecessors are being deleted. So today the group already drops them; the question is whether to switch to per-SHA + true.\n\nWith per-SHA + true: each SHA is its own group → nothing gets cancelled except same-SHA duplicates (re-runs). That eliminates mid-write cancels almost entirely, and each HEAD gets a verdict. That's actually appealing: per-SHA with true means a new push doesn't cancel the previous SHA's run because it's a different group. Cancels only happen when the same SHA is re-run. Hmm, but ci-gate has been per-SHA + true since 2026-10-08 — that's the precedent given.\n\nBut the semantic argument: the gate checks HEAD state of the register, not a per-SHA artifact. Hmm, careful: \"Das Gate prüft den HEAD-Zustand des Registers, kein je-SHA-Artefakt.\" — That means the result is defined on HEAD. If the group is per-SHA, then the run for SHA X checks... register state at that time. Since the group is per-SHA, concurrent pushes create parallel runs on different register states — race on the register file. Hmm.\n\nLet me think about what the strongest, defensible verdict is. The problem: with per-ref + true, a new push cancels the running run — mid-write risk per Kimi. With per-ref + false, a new push deletes waiting predecessors → you only get the latest (that is B's description: \"nur der jüngste Lauf lebt\"). Wait, B is \"per-ref + true (nur der jüngste Lauf lebt)\". Both A and B end up with only the newest living in some sense.\n\nActually the difference: per-SHA + true → all 25 SHAs get their own run, no cross-cancel; only same-SHA re-runs cancel. Per-ref + true → every new push cancels the running run regardless of SHA → mid-write kill risk, and you lose intermediate verdicts (but per the argument, the HEAD state is defined per SHA... hmm, \"der HEAD ist je SHA definiert\" — the GLM/DeepSeek/Lumo argument: a run on SHA X checks the register at that time — so each SHA's verdict is meaningful and worth computing. But then with per-SHA, you have parallel runs checking different register states — and the register is mutated by the lines? The register is a living document; writes by lines.\n\nGiven the gate checks the HEAD state of the register and not a per-SHA artifact, per-SHA creates N runs that all read the same (mutating) register at different times — the value of the older SHA's verdict decays as the register changes. Fugu/Mistral/Kimi: \"die Adresse des HEAD ist der Ref, per-SHA adressiert die Vergangenheit\" — hmm, that argues for per-ref.\n\nSo which? Given the gate result is only meaningful for the current HEAD of the register (which changes independent of the git ref), the per-SHA grouping doesn't isolate anything: two different SHAs at the same register state produce the same verdict; a group per SHA just prevents the useful coalescing. Conversely per-ref + true cancels the running run mid-write — risk per Kimi. Per-ref + false is the current state, which already coalesces waiting runs (7 of 8 cancelled = the coalescing works) and protects the running one from mid-write kills. So arguably the current setting is already the best and the answer is: keep per-ref, but... the options are A per-SHA+true, B per-ref+true, C debounce/batch. Hmm, \"C — Debounce/Batch\" — a batch job that runs once per interval: that would keep HEAD-only semantics and no cancel risk, plus it reduces the 5–7 gates fan-out? But C isn't offered with details; debounce = wait for a quiet period. That's a real option that avoids both mid-write cancels and history.\n\nHmm. I need to decide. The measured facts: 8 runs in 10 minutes, 7 cancelled → coalescing already happens effectively with `false` (pending ones dropped). So the actual loss from `false` is minimal. The only real defect of per-ref+false: waiting runs of *older* refs queue... Actually subtle: in GitHub concurrency, a run queued for an older ref in the same group is cancelled when a newer run is queued (even with false). So per-ref+false already gives \"only the newest lives\". The difference between false and true is whether the *running* one is killed.\n\nSo: B (per-ref + true) would additionally kill the running run → adds the mid-write risk with no gain, since the result is HEAD-only and the newest run re-checks everything. Therefore B is bad. A (per-SHA + true) keeps every SHA's verdict but the verdict is on a mutable register — parallel runs on different register states produce verdicts that are quickly meaningless (\"per-SHA adressiert die Vergangenheit\"), and it multiplies runs (25 SHAs / 100 runs already measured — with 5–7 gates per push, per-SHA would increase parallelism and cost). So A bad. C — debounce/batch: coalesces without cancelling the running one, keeps HEAD-only semantics. That's the one that addresses the actual defect (redundant runs per push, 5–7 gates) and avoids mid-write kills.\n\nHmm, but is C \"pending\"? The instruction: at uncertainty explicitly \"pending\" + what to measure is missing. I could say: C, but with a pending sub-decision: the ratio of reduntanten Läufen (how many of the 8 runs in 10 min were on a SHA that was superseded within the run) is not measured. Actually — I'm permitted to pick C and note pending.\n\nBut careful: does a debounce/batch solve \"5–7 gates per push\"? A batch that runs the coverage gate periodically (e.g., every 10 min) after a push would coalesce. It's honest: the evidence supports \"result is HEAD-only\" and \"cancel is dangerous\" → so neither A nor B; C. Verdict: C (with pending on the debounce window, needs measurement of run duration/p95 of the gate and the write duration of the register to set the quiet window safely). Also: regardless of the choice, the gate must ignore `cancelled` conclusions (Kimi) — that's an unconditional consequence.\n\nAlso note: the tie 3:3 means no majority; I should say clearly that I'm breaking the tie with a reason, and mark the window as pending.\n\nF3: register doesn't know state/rat. Options: A additional document root in the reader; B derive: verdicts migrate as `## An <linie>` blocks into the living handover of the recipient line; C new register document state/rat/_register.md (index only); D status quo manual distribution.\n\nVerdict: A — the reader is read-only; the lines may not change architecture but reading is not changing. Evidence: grep","role":"assistant"}}],"created":1792312798,"id":"f5f66c05013d70c2","model":"space-bunny:free","object":"chat.completion","usage":{"completion_tokens":2200,"prompt_tokens":1368,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":140},"total_tokens":3568}}